# 9. SCIENTIFIC SIGNAL-PROCESSING ENGINE

Implement the mathematical processing as reusable, testable modules rather than mixing every calculation into UI event handlers.

## A. Time-domain data

Maintain a collection of timestamped samples with consistent units.

Display:
- Time in seconds.
- Signal amplitude in m/s² for appropriately labelled acceleration data.
- Original or acquired samples.
- Selected analysis samples.
- Sampling interval.

Use timestamps rather than assuming all device events occur at perfectly uniform intervals.

For nonuniform live timestamps, explain that standard FFT interpretation assumes uniform sampling. Resample onto a uniform time grid using a documented interpolation method before computing a conventional FFT, or explicitly identify when the data is unsuitable for reliable FFT analysis.

## B. Sampling

Implement a clearly defined analysis sampling rate.

When demonstrating ideal discrete-time sampling with a known synthetic signal, generate or sample the signal at the selected target rate.

When working with acquired data, perform real timestamp-aware resampling or downsampling. Do not simply relabel an unchanged signal with a different sampling rate.

Explain the distinction between:
- Hardware acquisition rate.
- Observed browser event rate.
- Analysis sampling rate.
- Effective sampling interval.

If the input rate is insufficient to represent the requested analysis rate, show a warning rather than inventing additional information.

## C. Nyquist theorem

Calculate:

f_N = f_s / 2

For a band-limited signal, explain that ideal reconstruction requires a sampling frequency greater than twice the highest frequency present in the signal.

Use appropriate qualifications about signal bandwidth, anti-alias filtering, and practical limitations.

Do not imply that merely exceeding twice the dominant frequency is sufficient when higher-frequency components are also present.

## D. Aliasing demonstration

Implement a mathematically correct aliasing demonstration for known synthetic signals.

For a single sinusoid sampled at f_s, calculate the folded frequency using:

f_alias = |((f + f_s/2) mod f_s) - f_s/2|

Display:
- Original signal frequency.
- Analysis sampling frequency.
- Nyquist frequency.
- Predicted alias frequency.
- Whether the signal component lies above the Nyquist limit.
- A clear explanation of the observed behavior.

Use correct handling of frequency boundaries.

Do not claim that a visual warning alone proves aliasing. The plotted discrete samples and their corresponding frequency analysis must support the interpretation.

For composite signals, evaluate all known components rather than relying exclusively on a single dominant-frequency threshold.

Explain that aliasing cannot generally be undone once the relevant information has been lost.

## E. FFT / DFT

Implement a correct DFT or FFT.

Requirements:
- Use a trusted FFT library or a properly tested implementation.
- Apply a documented window function, such as Hann, when appropriate.
- Remove the mean only when the user enables detrending.
- Handle insufficient samples.
- Handle zero-length or constant signals.
- Use the correct frequency-bin calculation.
- Use a consistent amplitude normalization.
- Display a one-sided magnitude spectrum correctly.
- Distinguish magnitude from power spectral density.
- Clearly indicate the frequency resolution.

For uniformly sampled data:

Δf = f_s / N

The spectrum must use the actual effective sample rate and sample count of the processed data.

Avoid silently padding, truncating, or changing the sample rate in ways that produce misleading measurements. Document any zero-padding or resampling used.

## F. Signal metrics

Calculate and label:
- RMS value.
- Peak absolute amplitude.
- Peak-to-peak amplitude.
- Mean value.
- Observed event rate.
- Effective analysis sample rate.
- Dominant frequency.
- Nyquist frequency.
- Number of analyzed samples.
- Frequency resolution.

For detrended data, make it clear whether the displayed metrics use the raw or detrended signal.

Never label RMS as physical vibration severity or a calibrated measurement without appropriate calibration and context.

## G. Energy and power learning tools

Where applicable, add educational calculations for discrete-time energy and average power:

E = Σ |x[n]|²

P = lim as N → ∞ of (1/(2N+1)) Σ from n = -N to N of |x[n]|²

For finite captured sequences, label the calculated finite-window energy and average power as estimates over the selected observation window. Explain the difference between energy and power signals.

