# 21. TESTING AND SCIENTIFIC VALIDATION

Before declaring the project complete, test the application.

## Functional tests
- Every navigation item opens the correct view.
- Every button performs its stated action.
- Sliders and inputs update values and calculations.
- Start and stop controls work.
- Repeated starts do not create duplicate timers or listeners.
- CSV files contain valid headers and expected rows.
- Saved trials persist according to the selected storage strategy.
- Delete and reset actions work.
- Theme switching works.
- Mobile layouts remain usable.

## Scientific tests
- A 5 Hz sinusoid sampled at 40 Hz should be represented adequately under ideal conditions.
- A 15 Hz sinusoid sampled at 20 Hz should produce a predicted 5 Hz alias under ideal uniform sampling.
- The calculated Nyquist frequency must equal half the effective sampling rate.
- A sine wave's RMS should approach its amplitude divided by √2 when evaluated over a suitable number of complete cycles and without an additional DC component.
- A zero-mean constant-free test signal must return the expected metrics.
- FFT peak frequency must agree with the test signal within the limits imposed by frequency resolution and windowing.
- A low-rate analysis must not silently claim to recover frequency information that was not captured.

Use deterministic test signals for scientific tests rather than random noise.

Add unit tests for key mathematical functions when the environment supports automated testing.

Fix errors discovered during testing.

Do not claim that tests passed unless they were actually run.

